<p>This chimney was the last reminder of the recreation hall at Civilian Conservation Corps (CCC) Camp V2775, located at the former fairgrounds—now a baseball and softball complex—in southwest Mandan. The camp was staffed by World War I veterans, hence the “V” designation. Only three of the more than 250 CCC camps in the nation were reserved for veterans. A typical CCC company consisted of about 250 men ages 17 to 23. After another 45 men joined the Mandan camp through an October 1937 consolidation with the Fargo camp, the Mandan location became the largest CCC camp in North Dakota.</p>

<p>During their off-hours, CCC workers played cards, billiards, and table tennis or bought snacks at the recreation hall's canteen. A baseball field stood just outside, where one of Mandan's softball diamonds is today. At the National Association of CCC Alumni's annual reunion on July 15, 1989, the group placed a plaque made by member {% person "merlin-dahl" %}Merlin Dahl{% endperson %} in the chimney to commemorate its significance.</p>

