<p>The Civilian Conservation Corps operated from April 1933 through June 1942. The federal program sought to relieve widespread unemployment and hardship during the Great Depression by restoring natural resources and building public works such as parks and municipal utilities.</p>

<p>{% place "fort-abraham-lincoln" %}Fort Abraham Lincoln{% endplace %}, six miles south of present-day Mandan, was established in 1872 to protect area settlers and construction of the Northern Pacific Railroad. It was abandoned in 1891 after fulfilling its mission. In 1907, President {% person "theodore-roosevelt" %}Theodore Roosevelt{% endperson %} deeded the former fort's grounds to the State of North Dakota.</p>

<p>Park development began July 4, 1934, under the direction of the National Park Service in cooperation with the State Historical Society of North Dakota. Its primary aim was to preserve the area's history, including the Mandan Slant Indian Village and the ruins of Fort Abraham Lincoln's infantry and cavalry posts.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/gone-forever/ccc-camp-chimney/274-ft-a-lincoln-rvr-bottms.jpg" alt="Fort Abraham Lincoln grounds overlooking the Missouri River bottomlands">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>Major preservation projects included rebuilding the blockhouses and palisades at the infantry post; marking foundations and labeling building sites at both posts; rebuilding five Mandan earth lodges at the Slant Village site; and constructing a native-stone museum for historical displays. The reconstructed fort and state park buildings were dedicated July 17, 1938.</p>

<p>After completing their work at the state park, crews were redeployed to improve the City of Mandan's fairgrounds near their camp.</p>

<p>About 33,000 North Dakota men served in the CCC. Nationwide, the Depression-era program employed 3 million young men over nine years. Its workers built, installed, or restored 46,845 bridges, 800 state parks, 4,622 fish-rearing ponds, 5,000 miles of water-supply lines, and 3,462 beaches. They also relocated 45 million trees and shrubs and planted 3 billion trees.</p>

<p>The Mandan Park District demolished the chimney in 2024 as part of the baseball and softball complex expansion project.</p>

